1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,410
That sentence sounds too simple to be useful, and it is exactly why it is worth

2
00:00:05,410 --> 00:00:05,859
saying.

3
00:00:05,859 --> 00:00:11,047
People new to n8n expect the complexity to grow the same way spreadsheet formulas

4
00:00:11,047 --> 00:00:14,442
do, into something only the original author can read.

5
00:00:14,442 --> 00:00:15,211
It does not.

6
00:00:15,211 --> 00:00:19,887
A hundred-box workflow is still just boxes and lines, read left to right.

7
00:00:19,887 --> 00:00:22,385
The reason is structural, not cosmetic.

8
00:00:22,385 --> 00:00:27,638
A spreadsheet hides its logic inside cells: the sheet shows you results and buries

9
00:00:27,638 --> 00:00:28,535
the reasoning.

10
00:00:28,535 --> 00:00:33,659
The canvas is the opposite: every step is a visible box doing one action, in the

11
00:00:33,659 --> 00:00:37,118
order drawn, so the workflow is its own documentation.

12
00:00:37,118 --> 00:00:38,912
This matters beyond comfort.

13
00:00:38,912 --> 00:00:44,165
When Dev picks up Priya's templates in Section 10, he can read what they do before

14
00:00:44,165 --> 00:00:46,791
trusting them, without Priya in the room.

15
00:00:46,791 --> 00:00:51,083
When you inherit a workflow from a colleague who left, you can too.

16
00:00:51,083 --> 00:00:56,463
An automation your successor cannot read is a liability with a start button, and the

17
00:00:56,463 --> 00:00:59,410
canvas is what keeps these from becoming that.

18
00:00:59,960 --> 00:01:04,409
Go back to the Section 0 screenshot in your mind: trigger, agent, model.

19
00:01:04,409 --> 00:01:08,857
A trigger box, a node box, and a credential-holding node hanging off it.

20
00:01:08,857 --> 00:01:12,874
Four words, and you already had everything you needed to read it.

21
00:01:12,874 --> 00:01:17,878
The vocabulary earns its keep the day something fails, because the four words are

22
00:01:17,878 --> 00:01:19,361
also the four diagnoses.

23
00:01:19,361 --> 00:01:20,535
Nothing ran at all?

24
00:01:20,535 --> 00:01:24,737
Trigger: it is switched off, or the thing it watches never happened.

25
00:01:24,737 --> 00:01:26,343
One box red in the middle?

26
00:01:26,343 --> 00:01:30,545
Node: open it, read the last line of the error, fix that one action.

27
00:01:30,545 --> 00:01:33,449
Red on the box that talks to the outside world?

28
00:01:33,449 --> 00:01:37,712
Credential: the saved login expired or lost permission, reconnect it.

29
00:01:37,712 --> 00:01:42,717
The line between boxes never fails on its own, but clicking it shows you the data

30
00:01:42,717 --> 00:01:47,536
that flowed, which is how you catch the subtler case: every box green, and the

31
00:01:47,536 --> 00:01:52,294
answer still wrong, because a box was given something different from what you

32
00:01:52,294 --> 00:01:52,788
assumed.

33
00:01:52,788 --> 00:01:54,580
Four words, four first moves.

34
00:01:54,580 --> 00:01:57,484
That is most of the debugging you will ever do.

35
00:01:58,034 --> 00:02:02,768
Hold this picture and the earlier diagnosis list together and the canvas stops being

36
00:02:02,768 --> 00:02:04,684
a diagram and becomes a dashboard.

37
00:02:04,684 --> 00:02:06,657
Left edge: whether anything starts.

38
00:02:06,657 --> 00:02:08,404
Middle: what happens, in order.

39
00:02:08,404 --> 00:02:12,800
The box hanging underneath: where the outside world, and therefore most of the

40
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:13,871
failures, connects.

41
00:02:13,871 --> 00:02:18,380
When Section 3's build adds more boxes, they will all land in the middle of this

42
00:02:18,380 --> 00:02:21,479
picture, and nothing about how you read it will change.

43
00:02:22,029 --> 00:02:25,122
Notice the panel does not make you memorise anything.

44
00:02:25,122 --> 00:02:30,025
You type roughly what you want, "email," "sheet," "Gemini," and it narrows the list.

45
00:02:30,025 --> 00:02:33,993
That search habit alone gets you through every build in this course.

46
00:02:33,993 --> 00:02:38,837
Since the whole course runs on about ten node types, here is the map in one breath.

47
00:02:38,837 --> 00:02:42,864
Two triggers: one that watches an inbox, one that runs on a schedule.

48
00:02:42,864 --> 00:02:47,650
A handful of doers: read or write a sheet, fetch or send an email, post a message.

49
00:02:47,650 --> 00:02:52,436
The star pairing you already know: the AI Agent node with a model node underneath.

50
00:02:52,436 --> 00:02:57,221
And one piece of plumbing: a branch node that sends urgent left and routine right.

51
00:02:57,221 --> 00:03:01,248
Everything Priya builds, all four agents, is those pieces rearranged.

52
00:03:01,248 --> 00:03:06,034
When the panel shows you four hundred options, you are looking at a hardware shop,

53
00:03:06,034 --> 00:03:06,909
not a syllabus.

54
00:03:06,909 --> 00:03:08,018
You need one shelf.

55
00:03:08,568 --> 00:03:13,107
This is not a workaround, it is standard practice, and it is exactly how every

56
00:03:13,107 --> 00:03:17,413
workflow in this course was tested before a word of narration was written.

57
00:03:17,413 --> 00:03:21,138
Pin a sample, prove the logic, then connect the real thing once.

58
00:03:21,138 --> 00:03:23,233
The mechanics take one run to learn.

59
00:03:23,233 --> 00:03:27,248
Execute the workflow once for real, so the node has a genuine result.

60
00:03:27,248 --> 00:03:32,078
Pin that result, and from then on every test run downstream reuses it instantly and

61
00:03:32,078 --> 00:03:35,162
free, however many times you rewire what comes after.

62
00:03:35,162 --> 00:03:39,759
One warning, and it is the only sharp edge on this tool: a pin does not expire.

63
00:03:39,759 --> 00:03:44,240
A workflow that goes live with a pinned node keeps serving that frozen sample

64
00:03:44,240 --> 00:03:48,837
forever, with every box green, which is precisely the every box green and still

65
00:03:48,837 --> 00:03:50,234
wrong case from earlier.

66
00:03:50,234 --> 00:03:54,831
So adopt the closing ritual now, while it is cheap: before any workflow's trial

67
00:03:54,831 --> 00:03:57,275
starts, walk it once and unpin everything.

68
00:03:57,275 --> 00:03:59,719
Pins are for the workshop, never the road.

69
00:04:00,269 --> 00:04:05,463
One extra rep if you want it: n8n's own site hosts a public library of workflow

70
00:04:05,463 --> 00:04:07,370
templates other people built.

71
00:04:07,370 --> 00:04:12,761
Open any one of them and read it with the four words: find the trigger, follow the

72
00:04:12,761 --> 00:04:15,457
lines, spot which nodes hold credentials.

73
00:04:15,457 --> 00:04:20,913
Ninety seconds per workflow, and reading other people's canvases is exactly how the

74
00:04:20,913 --> 00:04:23,017
reading skill becomes automatic.

75
00:04:23,017 --> 00:04:24,727
That is the whole toolbox.

76
00:04:24,727 --> 00:04:30,118
Section 3 starts the real build: your first agent, from an empty canvas, screen by

77
00:04:30,118 --> 00:04:30,578
screen.
